Task Guide:

Task make_1_lvl1:
Propose a retrosynthesis route to synthesize the molecule with SMILES [CH2:1]=[C:2]([c:3]1[cH:4][cH:5][cH:6][cH:7][cH:8]1)[C@@H:9]1[CH2:10][CH2:11][CH2:12][C@@:13]1([OH:14])[C:15]([F:16])([F:17])[F:18]. The route must have at least 1 reactions.  You should use the template/s ['1914384'] in this order.

Required submission format:
Submit a JSON object representing the retrosynthesis route. It must follow the same JSON format as the next example: `{
  "type": "mol",
  "smiles": "CO",
  "children": [
    {
      "type": "reaction",
      "template_id": 1234,
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "mol",
          "smiles": "BrC"
        },
        {
          "type": "mol",
          "smiles": "[OH-]"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}`.



You must think what to do next. You can use some of the tools available in the system.

When you think that the task is completed, you can submit the answer.
For that, answer with: "Final Answer: <your answer>". It is very important to follow this format.

The single-child route is valid. This is chemically accurate as the intramolecular carbonyl-ene reaction has only one precursor. The atom mapping confirmed the transformation is correct, and the route passes schema validation.

Final Answer: 
```json
{
  "type": "mol",
  "smiles": "[CH2:1]=[C:2]([c:3]1[cH:4][cH:5][cH:6][cH:7][cH:8]1)[C@@H:9]1[CH2:10][CH2:11][CH2:12][C@@:13]1([OH:14])[C:15]([F:16])([F:17])[F:18]",
  "children": [
    {
      "type": "reaction",
      "template_id": 1914384,
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "mol",
          "smiles": "CC(=CCCCC(=O)C(F)(F)F)c1ccccc1"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

This route synthesizes the target via an intramolecular carbonyl-ene reaction (template 1914384). The single precursor `CC(=CCCCC(=O)C(F)(F)F)c1ccccc1` (a trifluoromethyl ketone bearing a trisubstituted styrenyl alkene) cyclizes: the ketone carbonyl is attacked by the alkene with allylic H-transfer, forming the cyclopentane ring with the tertiary CF3-carbinol and an exocyclic methylene group. The atom mapping was confirmed to align all atoms correctly with the target structure.
